## Reopened: this is now a blocker, not a tidiness task

Added when transcript publishing was planned (issues 029 and 030). The text
above is unchanged; this section says what has changed around it.

The transcripts are no longer only on disk. Double Diaper Dungeon's are mirrored
to a public GitHub repository on every push, and the plan is to render the
corpus as a website and publish it to Neocities. Publishing is the act that
makes an omission permanent: a page that was up is indexed and cached, and
taking it down afterwards does not take it back.

So this stopped being a cleanup that could wait and became the thing that gates
a deploy.

## What a first scan found, and why it settles nothing

Searching the live corpus by pattern:

| project | addresses | at-handles | notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| double-diaper-dungeon | none | none | only `/home/ritz`, which the text above explicitly permits |
| neocities-modernization | two | eight, and most are numeric ids rather than handles | the project this issue was raised about |

That looks reassuring and should not be trusted. A pattern search finds an
address because an address has a shape. **A bare name has no shape**, and the
person who raised this issue was reading the transcripts rather than grepping
them. The scan narrows where to look; it does not answer the question.

The distinction that matters for sequencing: the risk is **per project**, not
corpus-wide. A solo project's transcripts contain one person. A project that
processes other people's posts — which is what neocities-modernization does —
contains everybody it processed. Publishing the first is not the same decision
as publishing the second, and nothing should treat them alike.

## What this now needs, beyond the original text

1. **An audit that a person does, per project, before that project publishes.**
   Not a pattern scan. The scan is a tool for the person, not a substitute.
2. **The stripping system the text above describes**, still wanted, still
   per-project, still living beside the transcripts it governs.
3. **A refusal rather than a warning.** A publish step that finds no audit
   recorded for a project should stop, not proceed with a printed note. The
   house rule prefers a broken build to a silent fallback, and this is the case
   that rule exists for.
4. **A record of what was decided per project**, so the answer is not
   re-derived from memory each time and so a later reader can see that the
   question was asked at all.

